<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eshmunazar II</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eshmunazar II was a Phoenician king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> in the Achaemenid period, probably in the late 6th or early 5th century BCE. He is known from his black stone sarcophagus, found near Sidon in 1855 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, whose long inscription curses grave robbers and records that the Persian king granted Sidon the coastal towns of Dor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a>. It is a key source for the Phoenician language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii.jpg" alt="Eshmunazar II" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fred Romero from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eshmunazar_II_sarcophagus_(31289612892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
